#
# FILE            bridge_default_entity.test
#
# AUTHOR          Ken Zangelin
#
# Copyright 2026 Seamware
#
# A Channel says which endpoint becomes which attribute, and a sample on an
# endpoint no Channel claims is dropped. That is the right default - a
# transport that hands over everything it hears says a great deal the broker
# was never configured to want - but it is a poor way to FIND OUT what a system
# publishes, which is the first thing anyone connecting to one wants to know.
#
# So a bridge can be given a catch-all: one entity, where an unclaimed endpoint
# becomes an attribute named after itself. Point a broker at a live system with
# no mapping at all and everything on it shows up.
#
# ⚠ It is ON unless the configuration says "defaultEntity": false - as Orion-LD
# does for its DDS clients (KZ 2026-09-25). A broker that stores every endpoint
# it hears, unasked, is a different product - and on a busy domain an unbounded
# entity - so the off switch is tested too (05b).
#
# The samples come from the loopback bridge's emitAtStart, which is the only
# way to produce an unclaimed sample without a foreign publisher: everything
# else the loopback carries, it carries because a Channel asked it to.
#
# ⚠ mongoc, and for the same reason bridge_channel_prepopulate is: step 05
# reads values written before a restart, which asks the BACKEND to outlive the
# process. Under corDB they are gone for a reason that has nothing to do with
# the catch-all.
#
# REQUIRE_DB: mongoc
#
--NAME--
Bridges: endpoints no Channel claims land in the catch-all entity, when one is configured

--TAGS--
bridge channel plugins

--INIT--
corDbDrop

#
# Only the bridge traces, so the grep in step 04 cannot match anything else.
#
export COR_TRACE_LEVELS=402

#
# One claimed endpoint and two nobody claims. All three are emitted at start.
#
BRIDGE_CONFIG=$(bridgeConfig \
  --defaultEntity true \
  --topic "P1,Machine,urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1,temperature" \
  --emit "P1=41" \
  --emit "rt/chatter={ \"data\": \"Hello World: 7\" }" \
  --emit "humidity=63")

coraineStart --bridges loopback --bridgeConfig $BRIDGE_CONFIG

--RUN--

#
# ⚠ Again, and not by oversight: --INIT-- and --RUN-- are separate PROCESSES,
# so the export in --INIT-- reached the broker started there and nothing else.
# The restarts below are started from here, and without this their logs come
# out at the default trace levels - which is how step 06 first "passed" with
# no evidence in the log at all.
#
export COR_TRACE_LEVELS=402

echo "01. The claimed endpoint went where it always did"
echo "================================================="
corAwaitBody 10 /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1 41
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1
echo
echo


echo "02. And the other two are in the catch-all, named after their endpoints"
echo "======================================================================="
#
# urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default, of type LOOPBACK: the derived pair, which is
# the bridge's alias and nothing else. For the dds bridge that rule gives
# urn:ngsi-ld:dds:default of type DDS.
#
# Note rt/chatter: the endpoint becomes the attribute name as it is written,
# slash included. Nothing mangles it - it is what the publisher calls the
# thing, and the point of the catch-all is to show exactly that.
#
corAwaitBody 10 /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default humidity
corAwaitBody 10 /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default chatter
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default
echo
echo


echo "03. An attribute of the catch-all is NOT a Channel - it publishes nowhere"
echo "========================================================================="
#
# A Channel carries both ways. The catch-all only carries in: nothing said
# which endpoint an attribute of it belongs to, and inventing one would have
# the broker publishing to endpoints nobody configured. So the PATCH is an
# ordinary write and the trace below shows no publish for it.
#
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default/attrs/humidity -X PATCH --payload '{
  "type": "Property",
  "value": 99
}'
echo
#
# The same PATCH on the CLAIMED attribute, which does publish - without the
# contrast, step 04 being empty would prove only that nothing was written.
#
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1/attrs/temperature -X PATCH --payload '{
  "type": "Property",
  "value": 42
}'
echo
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default
echo
echo


echo "04. One publish, and it is the claimed endpoint's - not the catch-all's"
echo "========================================================================"
grep -oE "\-> '[^']+' on bridge 'loopback'" /tmp/coraine.CB.log | sort | uniq -c | sed 's/^ *//'
echo
echo



echo "05. Restart with no defaultEntity in the file - the default, ON: it is stored"
echo "=============================================================================="
#
# The same bridge, a new endpoint nobody claims, and no defaultEntity. This is
# what every deployment that does not say gets - the catch-all, as Orion-LD
# gives its DDS clients (KZ 2026-09-25).
#
BRIDGE_CONFIG2=$(bridgeConfig -o /tmp/coraine_bridges2.json \
  --topic "P1,Machine,urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1,temperature" \
  --emit "pressure=7")

coraineStart --bridges loopback --bridgeConfig $BRIDGE_CONFIG2

corAwaitBody 10 /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default '"pressure"'
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default
echo
echo



echo "05b. Restart with \"defaultEntity\": false - off, and it drops"
echo "=============================================================="
#
# A new endpoint nobody claims, and the file turning the catch-all off. The
# claim most likely to be broken by accident later: a broker that ignored the
# "false" would pass every other step in this file.
#
BRIDGE_CONFIG4=$(bridgeConfig -o /tmp/coraine_bridges4.json \
  --topic "P1,Machine,urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1,temperature" \
  --defaultEntity false \
  --emit "flowRate=5")

coraineStart --bridges loopback --bridgeConfig $BRIDGE_CONFIG4

#
# A fixed wait, deliberately: every other wait in this file is corAwaitBody on
# something that must APPEAR, and this one is waiting for something not to -
# which no condition can be polled for. A second is many times the delivery
# thread's 1 ms tick.
#
sleep 1
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default | grep -c '"flowRate"'
echo
echo



echo "06. A catch-all must not write an attribute a Channel already writes"
echo "===================================================================="
#
# defaultEntity pointed at the entity the Channel writes, and an unclaimed
# endpoint named exactly like the Channel's attribute. Both would write
# Machine:1/temperature, and the value would then depend on which arrived
# last - which is the collision channelCreate refuses when it can see it in
# the file. It cannot see this one: nothing named the endpoint.
#
BRIDGE_CONFIG3=$(bridgeConfig -o /tmp/coraine_bridges3.json \
  --defaultEntity "urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1,Machine" \
  --topic "P1,Machine,urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1,temperature" \
  --emit "temperature=1234")

coraineStart --bridges loopback --bridgeConfig $BRIDGE_CONFIG3
sleep 1
corCurl --url /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1
echo
#
# The value being untouched is the outcome; this is the REASON. Without it the
# step passes just as well when the sample was never delivered at all.
#
grep -oE "which channel '[^']+' already writes - dropped" /tmp/coraine.CB.log
echo
echo


--TEARDOWN--
coraineStop
rm -f /tmp/coraine_bridges.json /tmp/coraine_bridges2.json /tmp/coraine_bridges3.json /tmp/coraine_bridges4.json

--EXPECT--
01. The claimed endpoint went where it always did
=================================================
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Date: REGEX(.*)
Content-Type: application/json
Link: <https://uri.etsi.org/ngsi-ld/v1/ngsi-ld-core-context-v1.9.jsonld>; rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/json-ld#context"; type="application/ld+json"
Content-Length: 121

{
  "id": "urn:ngsi-ld:Machine:1",
  "temperature": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": 41
  },
  "type": "Machine"
}



02. And the other two are in the catch-all, named after their endpoints
=======================================================================
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Date: REGEX(.*)
Content-Type: application/json
Link: <https://uri.etsi.org/ngsi-ld/v1/ngsi-ld-core-context-v1.9.jsonld>; rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/json-ld#context"; type="application/ld+json"
Content-Length: 225

{
  "humidity": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": 63
  },
  "id": "urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default",
  "rt/chatter": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": {
      "data": "Hello World: 7"
    }
  },
  "type": "LOOPBACK"
}



03. An attribute of the catch-all is NOT a Channel - it publishes nowhere
=========================================================================
HTTP/1.1 204 No Content
Date: REGEX(.*)



HTTP/1.1 204 No Content
Date: REGEX(.*)



HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Date: REGEX(.*)
Content-Type: application/json
Link: <https://uri.etsi.org/ngsi-ld/v1/ngsi-ld-core-context-v1.9.jsonld>; rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/json-ld#context"; type="application/ld+json"
Content-Length: 225

{
  "humidity": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": 99
  },
  "id": "urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default",
  "rt/chatter": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": {
      "data": "Hello World: 7"
    }
  },
  "type": "LOOPBACK"
}



04. One publish, and it is the claimed endpoint's - not the catch-all's
========================================================================
1 -> 'P1' on bridge 'loopback'


05. Restart with no defaultEntity in the file - the default, ON: it is stored
==============================================================================
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Date: REGEX(.*)
Content-Type: application/json
Link: <https://uri.etsi.org/ngsi-ld/v1/ngsi-ld-core-context-v1.9.jsonld>; rel="http://www.w3.org/ns/json-ld#context"; type="application/ld+json"
Content-Length: 285

{
  "humidity": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": 99
  },
  "id": "urn:ngsi-ld:loopback:default",
  "pressure": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": 7
  },
  "rt/chatter": {
    "type": "Property",
    "value": {
      "data": "Hello World: 7"
    }
  },
